        "On August 19, Constitution met HMS Guerriere. The ships closed, exchanged fire, and became entangled. American shot damaged Guerriere's masts and hull. British fire struck Constitution too, but some balls were seen to rebound from its oak sides, the reported source of the name Old Ironsides. Guerriere was too badly damaged to bring into port and was burned after prisoners were removed.",
        "The physical advantage wasn't mythical. Constitution was larger, carried a heavier broadside, and had a numerous crew. American gunnery and handling converted those advantages into victory. British captain James Dacres faced a capable opponent, not an inexplicable failure of national character. A later court-martial acquitted him honorably after examining the condition and conduct of his ship.",
        "News traveled faster than qualification. Prints, poems, reports, and dinners made the duel a national event. The nickname made timber seem metallic and the vessel nearly invulnerable. Neither was true, but the material ship and the story now sailed together."
