        "Privateering imposed costs, raised insurance, diverted escorts, and created local fortunes. It also produced prisoners and exposed American sailors to British captivity. Its successes are difficult to convert into one measure because claims, recaptures, court decisions, and the difference between vessels taken and value destroyed vary across records. The commerce war mattered without becoming command of the sea.",
        "Britain answered with a blockade that grew tighter as ships became available. A merchant did not need to see a British frigate from the wharf if insurers, partners, and crews believed one waited beyond the headlands. The policy reduced customs receipts on which the federal government depended and confined much of the public navy. American ships could defeat British ships, but the United States could not force the Royal Navy away from its coast. The distinction would govern the maritime war."
